{
  "quest.18D51AA7C2E69779.quest_desc": [
    "The Low Heat Extruder only needs an HU input to conveniently process metals and some non-metals.",
    "Like the Crucible, it has no loss and never explodes, and it can make Pipes, Cables, Gears and Bent Plates in a single step.",
    "",
    "Unlike gt5, GT6's Low Heat Extruder supports almost every material form; the process just needs the input and output amounts to be equal and the material to be pure.",
    "&cNote: because of stack size limits, some recipes may lose material. When the input amount isn't divisible by the material amount of the output shape, it is rounded down and lost, e.g. 3 Tiny Piles of Dust -> 1 Ring.&r",
    "",
    "The Low Heat Extruder is the only lossless way to process Rubber and Plastic (the Crucible loses 1/3).",
    "",
    "The Low Heat Extruder needs Molds to work; there are steel low-heat-capacity Molds and Tungsten Carbide Molds.",
    "",
    "At this stage the steel Low Heat Extruder and steel Molds can only process some metals. The fully-fledged Extruder requires Tungsten Carbide.",
    "",
    "Special recipe: 1 Graphite Dust + Mold (Rod) -> 1 Graphite Rod (uses 512 HU/t).",
    "Yes, it can only turn Graphite into Rods - not into any other form.",
  ],
  "quest.35EEE7106152F82E.quest_desc": [
    "The Steam Engine is a machine that consumes Steam to produce KU.",
    "",
    "The Steam Engine has a buffer and needs some time to warm up; it only outputs once the stored energy reaches 20% of capacity.",
    "",
    "It can power any machine that accepts FE, with a conversion ratio of 1 KU : 2 FE (the 50% efficiency mentioned in the tooltip).",
    "",
    "At its rated output power it runs normally and outputs energy.",
    "The minimum output power is half the rated power; below that it can't keep working, and the warm-up takes longer.",
    "The maximum output power is twice the rated power; above that the buffered energy quickly hits the capacity limit and the machine shuts down.",
    "",
    "Its side can output Distilled Water equal to 80% of the consumed Steam.",
    "",
    "Note that this machine will NOT explode!!!",
  ],
  "quest.7DDFFC245F28FCFF.quest_desc": [
    "Keep right-clicking the Hand Crank with an empty hand to produce an uninterrupted 16 RU/t, at the cost of a little hunger.",
    "",
    "When a Villager's head hitbox overlaps the Crank's hitbox, the Crank starts working automatically.",
  ],
  "quest.2D2236E68060C731.quest_desc": [
    "This machine is an important early-game tool for ore doubling - build it as soon as you can.",
    "Note: some ores produce gem byproducts as well as Crushed Ore when put into the Crusher (for instance raw Redstone Ore yields Cinnabar), so keep this in mind when designing your line.",
    "",
    "It can also be used to farm Cobblestone.",
    "",
    "The machine needs at least 16 KU, so common setups are (ordered by power):",
    "1 Steel Boiler + Bronze Steam Engine; 2 Bronze Boilers + Bronze Steam Engine",
    "",
    "Early-game ore processing (Raw Ore to Dust) has 4 steps: Crushing, Washing, Centrifuging and Shredding.",
  ],
  "quest.3A464C578078C6CA.quest_desc": [
    "The Press uses KU to compress items.",
    "",
    "It can combine your items together; if you look at its JEI recipes, most of them are for gem-tipped pickaxe heads, bullets and arrows;",
    "",
    "But there's more: it can press Sawdust, Gunpowder and String into Dynamite for mining, or turn Plastic Plates and Sand into Circuit Plates.",
  ],
  "quest.1D9E330F71B12B74.title": "Some Properties of Steam",
  "quest.1F6C3C95EF83774E.quest_desc": [
    "The third step of ore processing.",
    "",
    "It is also the machine for processing ore dusts and for the Rubber line.",
  ],
  "quest.1536AE4183C21FBF.quest_subtitle": "The most practical thing in the Steam Age",
  "quest.1536AE4183C21FBF.quest_desc": [
    "Releases the fluid contained inside a container once the container is full.",
    "",
    "For liquids, a Drum must be attached in front. (It must be a Drum - a Pipe won't do!)",
    "",
    "For gases, attach a Drum in front, or expose it to the air.",
    "",
    "Together with a Drum it can output fluid in whole multiples of the Pipe's capacity.",
  ],
  "quest.1EE6D8205C7FBE5E.quest_desc": [
    "Consumes Steam to output RU (Rotational Energy).",
    "",
    "One of the most important machines in the early game.",
    "",
    "It's fairly expensive, so don't build many - 3 for your ore processing is enough; when other machines need one, borrow from there.",
    "",
    "The Steam Turbine won't explode.",
  ],
  "quest.659AFDEA608F0779.quest_desc": [
    "The automated Crucible / automated steelmaking system uses redstone control and a logistics design to feed in materials, extract output or make Steel automatically.",
    "",
    "Automated Crucible / steelmaking can be built around a small Crucible or a Large Crucible.",
    "",
    "Limited by capacity and heat mass, a small-Crucible setup isn't as fast as a Large Crucible, but idling it slowly is fine - and small Crucibles have a higher temperature limit than Large ones.",
    "So automated Crucible / steelmaking is usually built with small Crucibles.",
    "",
    "There are many ways to build the setup; they won't be covered here - search online.",
    "",
    "It's recommended to build both an automated Crucible and a Large Crucible.",
  ],
  "quest.4E74A94BCFE11D9F.title": "About Overclocking",
  "quest.4E6EBB2CAE8B81DF.title": "How to Properly Recycle Distilled Water",
  "quest.7392E2897605330F.title": "About Parallel Processing",
  "quest.22957BAC49E3EF47.quest_desc": [
    "Steelmaking requires a temperature above 2046 K, Wrought Iron, and Air pumped into the Crucible.",
    "",
    "Supplying KU to the side of the Crucible pumps in Air; there's no requirement on the input KU power, and the higher the KU, the more Air per pump. Note that nothing can be pumped in while the Crucible is full.",
    "",
    "Tip: a Hand Crank paired with a Rotation Engine produces a little KU, enough to smelt your first batch of Steel.",
  ],
  "quest.0D15FE6E63EAB039.quest_desc": [
    "Just put Rubber Pulp and a Plate Mold into the Low Heat Extruder.",
    "",
    "Rubber can make Fluid Pipes, but they can't carry hot fluids.",
    "",
    "Rubber can insulate Cables.",
    "",
    "Rubber can make a Rubber Hammer and a Plunger. The former lasts much longer than a wooden one, and the latter clears fluid out of Pipes.",
    "",
    "&bChapter 5: one of the unlock requirements for the Electric Age!&r",
  ],
  "quest.151C1E8C34FBBB29.quest_desc": [
    "An important machine for ore dust processing.",
    "",
    "The Roasting Oven turns sulfur-containing ore dusts into pure substances or oxides and produces Sulfur Dioxide.",
    "",
    "It yields more material than dumping the dust straight into a Crucible (Galena in a Crucible gives 0.333 Lead, while in a Roasting Oven it gives 0.351 Lead and 0.351 Silver).",
    "",
    "The Sulfur Dioxide produced can be used to make Sulfuric Acid, and the Roasting Oven is also a required machine for that.",
    "For now you don't need it though - just vent it into the atmosphere with a Pressure Valve. (GT6 has no pollution system.)",
    "",
    "The Roasting Oven needs Air or Oxygen; feeding it Air risks some loss, while Oxygen processes without loss.",
    "",
    "&bChapter 4: part of Chemical Engineering is now unlocked!&r",
  ],
  "quest.53BA0ADB5841C574.quest_desc": [
    "Too much Dirty Water? Make an Ender Garbage Bin to 'dispose' of it.",
  ],
  "quest.7392E2897605330F.quest_desc": [
    "A few machines show 'at most <number>x per run', which means they can perform parallel processing.",
    "",
    "The <number> in that hint is the maximum number of parallels it can handle at once.",
    "",
    "As long as there are enough input materials and output slots and no overflow would occur, the machine tries to consume extra inputs to start parallel operations. Each parallel thread consumes one batch of inputs and yields one batch of outputs, until all threads are full or adding another thread would overflow the output slots.",
    "",
    "When parallel processing happens, the total time for that run is single-recipe time * number of parallels, and the power stays the same.",
    "",
    "The Coke Oven has 16 threads, but its parallel processing doesn't increase the time, because it consumes no other energy - only time. A similar mechanic appears in the Large Bathing Vat.",
    "",
    "Parallel processing increases the time of a single run, which to some extent prevents silly overclocking.",
  ],
  "quest.1FCCA20EA255B5FF.quest_desc": [
    "A budget Melter: it can only melt furnace-smeltable materials, liquefy Calcite and make Hot Frying Oil, but is otherwise the same as the Melter.",
    "",
    "If you want to automate Molten Calcite, this machine is worth using.",
  ],
  "quest.1D9E330F71B12B74.quest_desc": [
    "Steam is an energy fluid in GT6, and GT6's fluid Drums cannot store it.",
    "(You can cheat by using a tank from another mod.)",
    "",
    "Steam is a gas, so it can't be transported in leaky Pipes such as Wooden Pipes.",
    "",
    "Pipes and GT Drums holding Steam will burn entities.",
  ],
  "quest.7DDFFC245F28FCFF.quest_subtitle": "Haha, wine fox!",
  "quest.53D189E8E7F4FD2A.quest_desc": [
    "Compared with the wooden Bathing Pot, the Stainless Steel Bathing Pot can hold high-temperature fluids.",
    "",
    "Its main use is holding Molten Zinc to produce Galvanized Steel, the material for LV machines.",
    "",
    "&bChapter 5: one of the unlock requirements for the Electric Age!&r",
  ],
  "quest.69612AA86A62883A.quest_desc": [
    "Don't be scared off by the Nichrome and Invar in the alloy smelting recipes.",
    "The real recipe is actually 6 Wrought Iron + 1 Chromium + 1 Nickel + 1 Manganese -> 9 Stainless Steel.",
    "To prevent alloy smelting recipe mix-ups, GT6 writes every possible intermediate product into the recipes.",
    "",
    "Chromium comes from Chromite, Nickel from Garnierite or Pentlandite, and Manganese from Pyrolusite.",
    "",
    "&bChapter 3: the Oil Age is unlocked!&r",
    "",
    "In theory you can already smelt Stainless Steel to build the Distillation Tower. But considering the cost of 320-odd Stainless Steel, 63 Copper and 36 Invar plus the complex part machining, it's recommended to push through the Steam Age first - at least get your ore processing set up.",
  ],
  "quest.783C84F7D1F8AE55.quest_desc": [
    "The fourth step of ore processing.",
    "",
    "The most expensive machine in early-game ore processing; it requires Crystalline Diamond Plates to craft.",
    "",
    "Since it works fast, it's recommended to add Hopper and Chest buffers on both the input and output.",
    "",
    "If you grow Wheat, it can also grind Flour.",
  ],
  "quest.18D51AA7C2E69779.quest_subtitle": "The Low Heat Extruder is king",
  "quest.2C11FF02E0B0FD74.quest_desc": [
    "A machine that processes Latex and produces Rubber Pulp.",
    "",
    "It's slow and can't be sped up by overclocking; build two.",
  ],
  "quest.48139B4FCAAFE58C.title": "Large Crucible",
  "quest.4E6EBB2CAE8B81DF.quest_desc": [
    "Both the Steam Turbine and the Steam Engine output Distilled Water equal to 80% of the consumed Steam from their sides.",
    "",
    "But piping it straight back into the Steam Boiler is a bad idea.",
    "",
    "If your Boiler burns ordinary Water, the Distilled Water can't get into the Boiler, so the return pipe does nothing and the water is wasted.",
    "In that case the right thing to do is attach a separate Drum.",
    "",
    "If the Boiler burns Distilled Water but is fed from a Distilled Water Drum, the Boiler will randomly draw Distilled Water from both pipes, which again wastes it.",
    "In that case the right thing to do is connect the return pipe to the Distilled Water Drum.",
    "",
    "If the Boiler is fed by a Dryer converting Distilled Water, it's recommended not to recycle the Distilled Water, because a system that uses redstone to check the Distilled Water level and toggle the Dryer is complicated and unnecessary.",
    "In that case only the large amounts of Distilled Water from a late-game multiblock Steam Turbine are worth recycling, and the Dryer can be toggled frequently.",
  ],
  "quest.659AFDEA608F0779.title": "Automated Crucible / Automated Steelmaking",
  "quest.787C96FF06D8E73B.quest_desc": [
    "A super-efficient Oven that works the same as a vanilla Furnace.",
    "",
    "It smelts exceptionally fast - 12.5 to 200 times faster than a vanilla Furnace! Build it as soon as you can!",
    "",
    "Each item smelted costs 256 GU.",
    "",
    "When smelting meat, supplying Cooking Oil (such as Fish Oil) consumes 50 L of Cooking Oil per operation and doubles the corresponding Cooked Meat output.",
  ],
  "quest.7AA635D03E727304.quest_desc": [
    "The second step of ore processing.",
    "",
    "Washes Crushed Ore with Water into Purified Ore, producing byproducts and Dirty Water.",
    "",
    "Unlike washing in a Cauldron, the Sluice produces more byproducts!",
    "The byproduct chance when washing in a Cauldron is only one ninth of that in the Sluice.",
    "",
    "Dirty Water can be centrifuged for some metals, but it takes a lot of power: the Centrifuge must have 4 times the power of the Sluice.",
    "Alternatively, a Dryer can dry it into Impure Dust for storage, but that takes a long time and isn't recommended.",
    "So early on it isn't worth processing Dirty Water - just attach an Ender Garbage Bin under the Sluice.",
    "",
    "The second most expensive machine in early-game ore processing.",
  ],
  "quest.460FFFF99C5D561B.quest_desc": [
    "The Drain can pump fluid blocks from the world into connected Pipes; it's a very important Pipe Cover.",
    "",
    "It can be attached to any Fluid Pipe, &cbut only works if the Pipe's capacity is 1000 L or more!&r",
    "",
    "In River and Lake biomes it can pump Water infinitely while touching a water block - the best pure-GT6 infinite Water source.",
    "",
    "In Ocean biomes it can pump Sea Water infinitely. In Swamp biomes it can pump Dirty Water infinitely - that's how you get infinite Dirt.",
    "",
    "It can also pump Oil and Natural Gas from the world into Pipes; it's the only way to use them.",
  ],
  "quest.171707E1A8688AAF.quest_desc": [
    "Congratulations - you have officially entered the Steam Age.",
    "",
    "You can build other kinds of Steam Boiler too, but before you get Steel, Bronze is recommended.",
    "Whichever you build, once you have Steel you can skip the quests before steelmaking.",
    "",
    "The Steam Boiler is a machine that consumes HU and converts HU into Steam.",
    "Every 1 L of Water converts into 160 L of Steam.",
    "Each litre of Steam requires 2 HU.",
    "",
    "Any kind of Water can be input, but non-distilled Water causes the Boiler to calcify and lose efficiency.",
    "",
    "The pressure gauge on the outside shows the Boiler's pressure; once it exceeds 100%, the Boiler explodes immediately and deals massive damage.",
    "",
    "The Boiler only outputs once its Steam storage exceeds 50%.",
    "The HU and Water consumed during the warm-up before that are swallowed - you have to pay the 'startup tax'.",
    "",
    "Assuming no calcification forms or builds up and the rated heat input is exactly reached, it takes 125 seconds in total to go from 0 Steam to being able to output Steam.",
    "",
    "A Chisel can remove calcification residue (efficiency returns to 100%, and both Steam and heat storage are reset to zero).",
    "But doing this while Steam remains inside makes it explode instantly.",
    "",
    "Unlike gt5, adding Water while dry-burning doesn't cause an explosion by itself, unless the Water added at once plus the stored heat is enough to push Steam pressure past 100% in an instant - only then does it overpressure-explode.",
    "When you notice dry burning, it's recommended to add Water in small amounts several times to bring the heat down.",
    "",
    "On the overclocking mechanic:",
    "",
    "HU  < 1x: the Boiler alternates between stopping and normal operation, and Steam storage settles at 50%",
    "HU  = 1x: the Boiler runs normally all the time, and Steam storage settles at 50%",
    "HU  > 1x and < 2x: the Boiler alternates between normal and overclocked operation, and Steam storage settles at 75%",
    "HU  = 2x: the Boiler runs overclocked all the time, and Steam storage settles at 75%",
    "HU  > 2x: Steam storage keeps rising until it explodes; the Boiler won't overclock any further",
    "",
    "Note that intermittent Steam output can't drive many machines well (the Steam Engine is the exception - it has its own buffer).",
    "It's best to use an HU input where the Boiler's rated HU is 1:1 or 2:1",
    "Otherwise you need a buffering method, such as throttling with Pipes.",
    "",
    "Most Steam Boilers run perfectly with a non-dense, non-fluidized-bed Burning Box of the matching material.",
    "The exceptions are the Chromium Boiler, which needs a Titanium Burning Box to run perfectly, and the Titanium Boiler, which needs a Chromium Burning Box to run perfectly.",
  ],
  "quest.721C438878CCA86E.quest_desc": [
    "Mainly used to make Screws without loss; you'll appreciate its importance once you build the Distillation Tower.",
  ],
  "quest.48139B4FCAAFE58C.quest_desc": [
    "The Large Crucible is a hollow 3×3×3 multiblock built mainly from Walls of the corresponding material - the multiblock version of the Crucible.",
    "",
    "A Large Crucible can be built from eight materials: Stainless Steel, Steel, Invar, Titanium, Tungsten, Tungstensteel, Adamantium and Tantalum Hafnium Carbide.",
    "",
    "A Large Crucible's temperature limit is lower than that of a small Crucible of the same material (about 1.1 times the material's melting point).",
    "",
    "The Large Steel Crucible is the most recommended early on and has the highest early temperature limit (2250 K), enough for most early- and mid-game materials.",
    "",
    "A Large Crucible has 27 times the capacity of an ordinary Crucible, holding 432 units at once. That lets you smelt a large number of items in one go.",
    "",
    "A Large Crucible also has a large heat mass, so a Chromium Fluidized Bed Burning Box (448 HU) or better is recommended; you'll soon unlock the Dense Chromium Fluidized Bed Burning Box (1792 HU).",
    "",
    "A Large Crucible needs a huge Mold system to operate, so prepare enormous numbers of Molds along with redstone for automated extraction.",
    "If you don't have enough Molds and extraction takes a long time, you can place an Arsenic Bronze Burning Box (28 HU) or one of similar power to keep it warm; this trick also applies to making Steel in a Large Crucible.",
    "",
    "Only a few hours of smelting are needed to gather the Steel Plates for a Large Steel Crucible. But the payoff is worth it.",
  ],
  "quest.0EAC002FDF401221.quest_desc": [
    "The Compressor uses KU to compress items into denser forms.",
    "",
    "It can compress your various Plates and Blocks into dense forms, and these dense plates give you more powerful equipment!",
    "",
    "It can also turn some simple Dusts into their Crystalline Plates, but only for rather loose material dusts such as Coal Coke.",
  ],
  "quest.2D2236E68060C731.quest_subtitle": "The start of ore processing",
  "quest.22957BAC49E3EF47.title": "Steelmaking",
  "quest.4E74A94BCFE11D9F.quest_desc": [
    "GT6's overclocking rule: each energy tier up multiplies the recipe's base energy requirement by 4 and the processing speed by 2. This is called lossy overclocking.",
    "",
    "For example, centrifuging Black Quartzite Dust needs 28 RU/t. With a Steel Centrifuge you need at least 112 RU/t of input power to process it; with only 64 RU/t it won't run, while the time needed is halved - effectively spending half the energy again.",
    "",
    "&cLossy overclocking isn't a reward, it's a penalty.&r You'll often run into the case where after overclocking the power is still not enough. GT6 encourages using machines that match the recipe's tier.",
    "",
    "Machines with the text 'overclocking does not lose energy efficiency' follow a different rule: each energy tier up multiplies the recipe's base energy requirement by 4 and the processing speed by 4.",
    "This overclocking doesn't lose energy efficiency, so it's called lossless overclocking; large machines and some single-block machines have this trait.",
    "",
    "Some recipes that straddle two energy tiers exactly, such as 64 GU/t or 256 GU/t, can be processed by machines of both tiers at the lowest energy input. For example, the 64 RU/t Dirty Water centrifuging recipe works in both the Bronze and Steel Centrifuge at 64 RU/t input, taking the same time and energy (since a 64 RU/t recipe counts as a tier-1 recipe in the Bronze Centrifuge and a tier-2 recipe in the Steel one, it isn't considered overclocking).",
    "",
    "&cOnce a recipe's runtime has already reached 1t, overclocking again won't reduce the time and just wastes energy. This situation is called 'silly overclocking'.&r",
  ],
}
